<SEOArticle title="Breathing Exercises for Athletes: Focus & Recovery" description="Practice simple breathing exercises for athletes before competition, between plays, after training and during recovery, with clear safety guidance." canonical="/mental-performance/breathing-exercises-for-athletes/" eyebrow="Mental Performance" heading="Breathing exercises for athletes: focus, reset and recover." lead="Breathing is not a magic switch, but it is always available. Practiced breathing can give an athlete a repeatable action before competition, during a reset and after training." {faqs} {toc} secondaryLabel="Mental Training Guide" secondaryHref="/mental-performance/mental-performance-training-for-athletes/">
 <p>Do not wait for the biggest moment to try a breathing technique for the first time. Practice during low-pressure training so the routine feels familiar when attention and emotion are elevated.</p>
 <h2 id="exercises">Four practical breathing exercises</h2>
 <h3>1. Comfortable diaphragmatic breathing</h3><p>Place one hand around the lower ribs or abdomen. Breathe quietly through the nose if comfortable and notice expansion around the lower trunk. Avoid forcing the largest possible breath. Practice for one to three minutes.</p>
 <h3>2. Longer-exhale breathing</h3><p>Inhale comfortably, then let the exhale last slightly longer. Do not strain or empty the lungs aggressively. Use several cycles when trying to downshift before performance or after a demanding effort.</p>
 <h3>3. Box-style pacing</h3><p>Use equal, comfortable phases for inhale, pause, exhale and pause. Start with short counts and remove the holds if they create tension or dizziness. The structure can help anchor attention.</p>
 <h3>4. One-breath reset</h3><p>Between plays or attempts, use one deliberate exhale, release unnecessary tension, name one task cue and return attention to the next action.</p>
 <div class="callout"><strong>Simple reset:</strong> exhale, soften the shoulders, choose one cue, act.</div>
 <h2 id="when">When athletes can use breathing</h2>
 <div class="grid two">
  <div class="card"><h3>Before competition</h3><p>Use a familiar pattern as part of the warmup—not as an emergency attempt to erase every nerve.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>Between attempts</h3><p>One controlled breath can mark the end of the previous play and the beginning of the next decision.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>After training</h3><p>Quiet breathing can help transition from high effort into cooldown and reflection.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>Before sleep</h3><p>A comfortable, non-straining routine may become part of a consistent wind-down process.</p></div>
 </div>
 <h2 id="routine">A five-minute pre-competition routine</h2>
 <ol><li>Notice current energy and tension without judging it.</li><li>Use 60 to 90 seconds of comfortable breathing.</li><li>If over-aroused, allow the exhale to become slightly longer.</li><li>Visualize one realistic opening situation.</li><li>Choose one short technical or tactical cue.</li><li>Finish with normal breathing and begin the physical warmup.</li></ol>
 <h2>Breathing for teams and coaches</h2>
 <p>Coaches can teach a shared reset without requiring every athlete to respond identically. Explain the purpose, practice briefly and allow individual variation. Breathing should not be used to blame athletes for normal emotion or replace mental-health support.</p>
 <h2 id="safety">Breathing safety</h2>
 <ul><li>Stop if you become dizzy, faint, numb or unusually uncomfortable.</li><li>Do not perform breath holds in water or while driving.</li><li>Avoid aggressive over-breathing without qualified supervision.</li><li>People with relevant medical conditions should seek individualized guidance.</li></ul>
 <h2>Connect breathing to mental performance</h2>
 <p>Breathing becomes more useful when paired with attention, imagery, self-talk and reflection. See the complete <a href="/mental-performance/mental-performance-training-for-athletes/">mental performance training guide</a>.</p>
